# Optimization API Reference

The optimization tools of PlussMa live in `pluss::subsp::math`. Each solver takes its problem as `Series` data, returns a result record you can read field by field, and reports in that record why it stopped, so a run that did not converge is a value to inspect rather than an exception.

```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

namespace roots = pluss::subsp::math::roots;
namespace qp = pluss::subsp::math::qp;
namespace linprog = pluss::subsp::math::linprog;
namespace milp = pluss::subsp::math::milp;
namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
namespace dfo = pluss::subsp::math::optimization;
```

Every page of this reference documents one function, method, enumeration or options structure: what it is, when to call it, every parameter and field, what it returns, what it refuses, and programs with real output that cover each overload and each option.

Not sure which solver fits your problem? The {guide}`optimization guide <optim>` opens with a chooser.

## The families

| Family | What it covers |
|---|---|
| [Root finding](optim/roots.md) | Bracketing and derivative-based solvers for a scalar equation |
| [Quadratic programming](optim/qp.md) | Convex quadratic programs, batches, warm starts, prepared solvers |
| [Linear programming](optim/linprog.md) | Interior-point and simplex linear programming with certificates |
| [Mixed-integer linear programming](optim/milp.md) | Branch-and-bound over integer variables |
| [Constrained optimization (Optima)](optim/optima.md) | Nonlinear programs: problem builders, four algorithms, options, results, reports |
| [First-order and quasi-Newton optimization (Septima)](optim/septima.md) | Adam, L-BFGS and L-SR1: options, constraints, results, standalone Adam steps |
| [Derivative-free search](optim/dfo.md) | The `DFO` front door and builder, Nelder-Mead, pattern search, differential evolution, swarms, mesh-adaptive search |

## Conventions

- Examples print with `pluss::tils::OutputEngine`; a `Series` is read with `.items<double>()` for a vector and `.item<double>()` for a single number.
- Constraints are written `g(x) ≤ 0` and `h(x) = 0`; linear rows are `A x ≤ b` and `A x = b`; infinite bounds mean no bound.
- A run that stops without converging returns normally with a status or exit flag. Check it before using the answer.
- Errors are `pluss::Error`; the message names the operation and the problem.

